at's what it's  supposed to do it's also kind of  interesting because there's some things  that they consciously decided not to  bring back into the first game for  example the downstate crawl was absent  yeah that's odd because I actually liked  it in the later games I felt like it  gave you a lot of strategy even when you  were down you could crawl behind cover  if someone's shooting at you from a  distance you could go towards your  teammates and sort of help them out when  they're trying to pick you back up and  it felt very jarring not to have that  because I felt really helpless and I  think I still instinctively was just  a when I went down and as far as things  they did bring back you can also mark  enemies to show your teammates where  they are if they're not sure that's  another thing that came along in the  later sequels after the original 360  game and I think that's a good thing to  bring back I think it kind of helps your  team coordinator where the enemies are  it kind of improve your communication if  you're not necessarily just relying on  the microphones which of course is  always great anyway but it just gives  you another layer to how you communicate  with your team and I think it was a good  call to bring it back it felt great it  helped me out I think it helped my  teammates out in the long run we also  got a chance to check out single player  we played co-op so I love the my old  favorite weapons such as the hammer of  dawn it felt very similar to me as the  old I mean it was graphically upgraded  but again it felt very classic they did  say that they added what an extra two  hours of content that was only on PC for  the Ultimate Edition yeah there was  these two hours late in the game that  was only ever only ever came out on the  PC version that never made it to console  so this is technically the first time  it's ever on consoles with the Xbox one  version and we played a little bit of  that they kind of ended us right at up  this boss fight with a brew Mac that  people have played on PC will remember  but yeah other than that it doesn't feel  too much different I mean the first  Gears of War people always said that the  campaign was much more horror inspired  than the following two even though two  and three we're both very dark as well  but it definitely feels like they're  going back to that more confined setting  for the most part and you know there's  like the hammer of dawn sequences I  didn't really notice too much stutter  there are a few points I think when we  were fighting larger enemies when we  first saw the brew Mac on screen and the  Reavers in the air and using the hammer  of dawn like you mentioned it kind of  showed a little bit of stutter but  nothing that was too invasive and it  yeah I just felt like the original Gears  of War just obviously looks much better  and feels a little smoother which is  nice now that the Ultimate editions been  announced it's a little bit strange  timing because backwards compatibility  for Xbox one was also just announced how  do you feel about buying the Ultimate  Edition versus maybe just waiting for  the backwards compatibility for the  original gears two and three well I  think there are a few reasons it just  feels better especially in the  competitive multiplayer maps it's nice  to have 60 frames per second it's nice  to have these maps that look gorgeous  compared to what they used to they kind  of got rid of the  really dark gray muddy texture that kind  of pervaded the original game it's also  funny you mentioned backwards  compatibility because if you buy Gears  of War Ultimate Edition for $40 and play  it before New Year's Eve this year  you'll also get the Xbox 360 versions of  Gears of War 2 Gears of War 3 and Gears  of War judgment on Xbox one as part of  Microsoft's backwards compatibility plan  awesome thanks Mike  no problem
 

